[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
**Short answer:** Yes. You can embed a full MapLibre-GL style (vector-tile basemap, optional terrain, sprites, glyphs, etc.) inside a Leaflet map by using the **MapLibre GL Leaflet** plugin. The plugin creates a Leaflet layer that internally runs a MapLibre GL JS map, so all of the vector-tile styling capabilities stay intact while the rest of your UI continues to use Leaflet.

Below is a step-by-step guide, plus the “gotchas” you’ll run into most often.

---

## 1. What the plugin does (and why it’s the right tool)

| Leaflet core | MapLibre GL core | MapLibre GL Leaflet plugin |
|--------------|------------------|----------------------------|
| Draws **raster** tiles only (PNG/JPEG/WebP) | Renders **vector** tiles, terrain, text, symbols, and lets you query features client-side | Wraps a hidden MapLibre GL canvas inside a Leaflet `L.GridLayer`. The canvas is positioned exactly where Leaflet expects a raster tile, but the content is rendered by MapLibre GL. |
| No built-in style language | Uses the MapLibre **style JSON** (layers, sources, paint/layout) | Accepts a full style URL or an inline style object, exactly like a native MapLibre map. |
| No feature-level interactivity (except via separate GeoJSON layers) | Click/hover/query on vector features | You can still use MapLibre’s `queryRenderedFeatures` through the plugin’s `maplibreMap` property, or you can add separate Leaflet GeoJSON layers on top. |

Because the plugin treats the MapLibre map as a single Leaflet layer, you keep all the Leaflet conveniences you already use (controls, CRS handling, other raster overlays, etc.) while gaining the power of vector tiles.

---

## 2. Install the required libraries

```html
<!-- Leaflet core (any recent 1.x version) -->
<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.css" />
<script src="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.js"></script>

<!-- MapLibre GL JS (>= 2.4.0, same version you’d use for a native map) -->
<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" />
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>

<!-- MapLibre GL Leaflet plugin (keeps versions in sync) -->
<script src="https://unpkg.com/@maplibre/maplibre-gl-leaflet@0.0.13/dist/leaflet-maplibre-gl.js"></script>
```

> **Version tip:** Keep the MapLibre GL version and the plugin version compatible. The plugin’s README lists the supported GL version range. If you upgrade MapLibre GL later, bump the plugin to the matching release.

---

## 3. Create a Leaflet map as usual

```javascript
// Basic Leaflet map (choose any CRS you need; most basemaps use EPSG:3857)
const map = L.map('map', {
  center: [40.7128, -74.0060], // New York City
  zoom: 12,
  // optional: set maxZoom/minZoom to match your vector tile source
});
```

---

## 4. Add the MapLibre-GL layer

You have two ways to give the plugin a style:

| Option | When to use |
|--------|-------------|
| **Style URL** (e.g. `https://api.maptiler.com/maps/streets/style.json?key=YOUR_KEY`) | You already have a hosted style (MapTiler, OpenFreeMap, your own TileServer, etc.). |
| **Inline style object** | You want to tweak the style on the fly, add custom sources, or embed everything in a single HTML file. |

### 4.1 Using a hosted style URL (the most common case)

```javascript
// Create the MapLibre GL Leaflet layer
const glLayer = L.maplibreGL({
  // The style can be a URL or a full JSON object
  style: 'https://api.maptiler.com/maps/streets/style.json?key=YOUR_MAPTILER_KEY',
  // Optional: pass MapLibre GL options (e.g., preserveDrawingBuffer for screenshots)
  // maplibreOptions: { preserveDrawingBuffer: true }
}).addTo(map);
```

### 4.2 Using an inline style (e.g., to add your own vector source)

```javascript
const myStyle = {
  version: 8,
  sources: {
    // Basemap source – you can point to any vector tile service
    basemap: {
      type: 'vector',
      url: 'https://api.maptiler.com/tiles/v3/tiles.json?key=YOUR_KEY'
    },
    // Your own data – could be a PMTiles URL, a TileJSON endpoint, or a GeoJSON source
    myData: {
      type: 'geojson',
      data: '/data/my-points.geojson'   // or a URL to a remote GeoJSON file
    }
  },
  layers: [
    // Basemap background
    { id: 'bg', type: 'background', paint: { 'background-color': '#e0e0e0' } },

    // Example basemap layers (roads, water, etc.) – copy from the source style or keep empty
    // ...

    // Your overlay – draw it *before* the first symbol layer so labels stay on top
    {
      id: 'my-points',
      type: 'circle',
      source: 'myData',
      paint: {
        'circle-radius': 6,
        'circle-color': '#ff6600',
        'circle-stroke-width': 1,
        'circle-stroke-color': '#fff'
      }
    }
  ]
};

const glLayer = L.maplibreGL({
  style: myStyle
}).addTo(map);
```

> **Layer-order tip:** If you’re mixing a vector basemap with your own overlay, insert your overlay **before** the first symbol layer (labels) so that the basemap’s labels stay readable. The plugin does not automatically reorder layers; you have to do it in the style JSON (or use the `insertBefore` helper described in the `maplibre-source-wiring` skill).

---

## 5. Interacting with the MapLibre map from Leaflet

The plugin exposes the underlying MapLibre GL map via the `maplibreMap` property:

```javascript
// Example: query features on click
map.on('click', function (e) {
  const point = map.latLngToContainerPoint(e.latlng);
  const features = glLayer.maplibreMap.queryRenderedFeatures([point.x, point.y], {
    // optional: filter by source-layer or layer id
    layers: ['my-points']
  });

  if (features.length) {
    const props = features[0].properties;
    alert('Clicked feature: ' + JSON.stringify(props));
  }
});
```

You can also call any MapLibre GL API (`setPaintProperty`, `addLayer`, `removeSource`, etc.) on `glLayer.maplibreMap`. This is handy for dynamic styling or toggling layers without rebuilding the Leaflet map.

---

## 6. Handling CORS & authentication

| Issue | Fix |
|-------|-----|
| **CORS errors** when fetching vector tiles or the style JSON | Ensure the tile server sends `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain). If you control the server, add the header; otherwise use a proxy or a provider that already supports CORS (MapTiler, OpenFreeMap, etc.). |
| **API keys in URLs** (e.g., `?key=…`) | Keep the key out of public source code when possible. You can inject it at runtime (`style: styleUrl.replace('{key}', myKey)`) or use a server-side proxy that adds the key. |
| **Token-based auth (Bearer, signed URLs)** | MapLibre GL supports `headers` in the source definition. When you build an inline style, add a `headers` object to the source: <br>`headers: { Authorization: 'Bearer <token>' }`. The plugin passes those straight through. |

---

## 7. Adding other Leaflet layers on top (optional)

Because the MapLibre layer is just another Leaflet layer, you can stack raster, image, or video layers above or below it:

```javascript
// A raster overlay (e.g., a weather radar)
L.tileLayer('https://tiles.example.com/radar/{z}/{x}/{y}.png', {
  opacity: 0.6,
  zIndex: 500   // higher than the GL layer (default 400)
}).addTo(map);

// A Leaflet GeoJSON layer (if you need simple point markers that aren’t vector-styled)
L.geoJSON(myGeojson, {
  pointToLayer: (feature, latlng) => L.circleMarker(latlng, { radius: 4, color: '#00f' })
}).addTo(map);
```

The draw order follows Leaflet’s `zIndex` (or the order you add layers). The MapLibre GL layer respects its own internal layer order for the vector tiles, but you can still control the overall stacking with Leaflet.

---

## 8. Performance & mobile considerations

| Factor | Recommendation |
|--------|----------------|
| **Tile size** | Vector tiles are usually 10–30 KB per tile (z0-z14). Keep the source’s `maxzoom` reasonable for mobile (e.g., 14–15). |
| **GPU memory** | The hidden MapLibre canvas uses the same WebGL context as a native MapLibre map. On low-end phones, limit the number of concurrent layers (especially heavy symbol layers). |
| **Feature count** | If you add a large GeoJSON source (≥ 5 k features) directly, you’ll hit the same thresholds described in the *GeoJSON performance* section of the skill. Prefer a vector tile source for > 5 k features. |
| **Terrain** | Adding a `raster-dem` source for hillshading works, but it adds extra texture memory. Only enable it if you need 3-D terrain. |
| **Offline** | The plugin can work with locally stored tiles (e.g., PMTiles served via `pmtiles://` URLs) – see the `maplibre-pmtiles-patterns` skill for a server-less setup. |

---

## 9. Full minimal example (copy-paste)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>Leaflet + MapLibre GL Vector Basemap</title>
  <style>#map { height: 100vh; width: 100%; }</style>

  <!-- Leaflet -->
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.css"/>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.js"></script>

  <!-- MapLibre GL -->
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css"/>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>

  <!-- MapLibre GL Leaflet plugin -->
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/@maplibre/maplibre-gl-leaflet@0.0.13/dist/leaflet-maplibre-gl.js"></script>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  // 1️⃣ Leaflet map
  const map = L.map('map', {
    center: [40.7128, -74.0060],
    zoom: 12,
    maxZoom: 18,
    minZoom: 2
  });

  // 2️⃣ MapLibre GL layer (hosted style)
  const glLayer = L.maplibreGL({
    style: 'https://api.maptiler.com/maps/streets/style.json?key=YOUR_MAPTILER_KEY'
  }).addTo(map);

  // 3️⃣ Optional: click-to-query example
  map.on('click', function (e) {
    const pt = map.latLngToContainerPoint(e.latlng);
    const feats = glLayer.maplibreMap.queryRenderedFeatures([pt.x, pt.y], {
      // limit to a layer you know exists, e.g. 'road' or 'building'
      layers: ['road', 'building']
    });
    if (feats.length) {
      console.log('Feature properties:', feats[0].properties);
    }
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Replace `YOUR_MAPTILER_KEY` with a valid token (or use any other vector-tile style URL that supports CORS). The map will behave exactly like a native MapLibre GL map, but you can still add Leaflet controls, markers, or other raster overlays as you normally would.

---

## 10. Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Blank map** (white canvas) | Style URL not reachable, CORS blocked, or missing `accessToken`/`key`. | Open the style URL directly in the browser → check network tab for CORS errors. |
| **Labels disappear after adding a Leaflet raster layer** | Raster layer added **above** the GL layer (higher `zIndex`). | Set `zIndex` of the raster layer lower than the GL layer, or add the raster layer **before** `glLayer`. |
|

