**Short answer:** Yes. You can embed a full MapLibre-GL style (vector-tile basemap, optional terrain, sprites, glyphs, etc.) inside a Leaflet map by using the **MapLibre GL Leaflet** plugin. The plugin creates a Leaflet layer that runs a hidden MapLibre GL JS canvas and composites it with the rest of your Leaflet layers, so the rest of your Leaflet code stays unchanged.

Below is a complete, step-by-step guide that covers:

1. **Why you need the plugin** (Leaflet alone only draws raster tiles)  
2. **Installing the plugin** (npm, CDN, or bundler)  
3. **Creating the MapLibre GL layer** (style URL, source options, CORS)  
4. **Placing your own Leaflet layers** (order, interaction)  
5. **Common gotchas** (attribution, retina tiles, sprite/glyph URLs, offline)  
6. **Optional extras** (terrain DEM, custom GeoJSON overlay, PMTiles)  

---

## 1️⃣  Background – Leaflet vs. MapLibre GL

| Library | What it renders natively | Typical source type |
|---------|--------------------------|---------------------|
| **Leaflet** | Raster tiles (PNG/JPEG/WebP) drawn with a `<canvas>` or `<img>` element | `raster` (or `image`/`video`) |
| **MapLibre GL JS** | Vector tiles (or raster-DEM) rendered with WebGL, full style engine | `vector`, `raster-dem`, `geojson` (client-side) |

If you only add a raster tile URL to Leaflet you lose:

* **Resolution-independent styling** (labels stay crisp at any zoom/DPI)  
* **Feature-level interactivity** (click/hover on individual road segments, dynamic paint)  
* **Terrain rendering** (hillshade, 3-D extrusion)  

The **MapLibre GL Leaflet** bridge gives you the best of both worlds: a Leaflet map that can host a *MapLibre style* as a single layer.

---

## 2️⃣  Install the bridge

### Option A – CDN (quick prototype)

```html
<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.css" />
<script src="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.js"></script>

<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" />
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/@maplibre/maplibre-gl-leaflet@0.0.13/dist/leaflet-maplibre-gl.js"></script>
```

> **Version tip:** Keep the three libraries roughly in sync (Leaflet 1.9+, MapLibre GL 4.x, maplibre-gl-leaflet 0.0.13+). Check the plugin’s README for the exact compatible matrix.

### Option B – npm / bundler (production)

```bash
npm i leaflet maplibre-gl @maplibre/maplibre-gl-leaflet
```

Then in your JavaScript (ESM or CommonJS):

```js
import L from 'leaflet';
import 'leaflet/dist/leaflet.css';

import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';

import { MapLibreGL } from '@maplibre/maplibre-gl-leaflet';
```

> **Why a bundler?** You get tree-shaking, easier control over CSS loading, and can replace the default `fetch` implementation (e.g., to add authentication headers).

---

## 3️⃣  Create the Leaflet map and add the MapLibre layer

```js
// 1️⃣  Initialise the Leaflet map (any CRS you like – default EPSG:3857 works)
const map = L.map('map', {
  center: [40.7128, -74.0060], // New York City
  zoom: 12,
  // optional: set maxZoom/minZoom to match your vector tile source
  maxZoom: 18,
  minZoom: 5,
});

// 2️⃣  Add a regular Leaflet base layer (optional – you can keep it empty)
L.tileLayer('https://{s}.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png', {
  attribution: '&copy; OpenStreetMap contributors',
}).addTo(map);

// 3️⃣  Add the MapLibre GL layer
const mapLibreLayer = new MapLibreGL({
  // a full MapLibre style URL (hosted or self-hosted)
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
  // If you need to pass extra options to the internal maplibre-gl instance:
  maplibreOptions: {
    // Example: enable terrain if the style references a raster-dem source
    // (requires the style to have a "terrain" entry)
    // terrain: { source: 'terrainSource', exaggeration: 1.5 },
    // Example: add a custom access token header for private tile services
    // transformRequest: (url, resourceType) => ({
    //   url,
    //   headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${myToken}` },
    //   credentials: 'include',
    // }),
  },
  // Control how the GL canvas blends with Leaflet layers
  // (default is 'additive' which works for most basemaps)
  // See plugin docs for 'additive', 'multiply', 'normal', etc.
  // blendMode: 'additive',
}).addTo(map);
```

### What the plugin does under the hood

| Step | Description |
|------|-------------|
| **Canvas creation** | A hidden `<canvas>` is created and handed to MapLibre GL. |
| **WebGL rendering** | MapLibre draws the vector tiles, terrain, labels, etc. |
| **Leaflet compositing** | The canvas is added as a Leaflet `L.GridLayer`-like overlay, so it participates in Leaflet’s pan/zoom events. |
| **Sync** | The plugin keeps the MapLibre view (`center`, `zoom`, `bearing`, `pitch`) in lockstep with Leaflet’s `viewreset`, `move`, and `zoom` events. |

Because the GL canvas is just another Leaflet layer, you can still add **any number of regular Leaflet layers** (markers, polylines, GeoJSON, heatmaps) on top or below it.

---

## 4️⃣  Adding your own data on top of the basemap

### 4.1 Leaflet-only data (most common)

```js
// Example: a GeoJSON feature collection of store locations
fetch('/data/stores.geojson')
  .then(r => r.json())
  .then(geojson => {
    L.geoJSON(geojson, {
      pointToLayer: (feat, latlng) =>
        L.circleMarker(latlng, { radius: 6, fillColor: '#ff6600', color: '#fff', weight: 1, opacity: 1, fillOpacity: 0.9 })
    })
    .addTo(map);
  });
```

*These layers sit **above** the MapLibre GL canvas by default because they are added after the GL layer.*

### 4.2 Vector-tile overlay (if you have many points)

If you cross the GeoJSON thresholds (≈ 5 k features) you can also add a **second MapLibre source** (vector tiles) to the same style. The plugin lets you **mutate the style at runtime**:

```js
// Assume you already have a MapLibreGL instance called mapLibreLayer
const glMap = mapLibreLayer.getMap(); // returns the internal maplibre-gl Map object

// Add a new source (vector tiles hosted on your server)
glMap.addSource('my-points', {
  type: 'vector',
  url: 'https://mytiles.example.com/tileset.json' // TileJSON endpoint
});

// Add a layer that draws the points
glMap.addLayer({
  id: 'my-points-circle',
  type: 'circle',
  source: 'my-points',
  'source-layer': 'points', // name inside the tileset
  paint: {
    'circle-radius': 5,
    'circle-color': '#ff6600',
    'circle-stroke-width': 1,
    'circle-stroke-color': '#fff'
  }
});
```

*Because the style lives inside the same GL map, the points are rendered **in the same WebGL canvas**, preserving crispness and performance.*

---

## 5️⃣  Practical considerations & gotchas

| Issue | What to watch for | Fix / Recommendation |
|-------|-------------------|----------------------|
| **CORS** | Vector tile URLs, sprite URLs, glyph URLs must allow cross-origin requests (or be on the same origin). | Serve TileJSON with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` or your domain. For self-hosted sprites/glyphs, add the header. |
| **Attribution** | Leaflet’s built-in attribution control only knows about Leaflet layers. | Use `mapLibreLayer.addTo(map);` then call `mapLibreLayer.addAttribution('© OpenMapTiles © OpenStreetMap contributors');` or manually add to `L.control.attribution`. |
| **Retina / Hi-DPI** | MapLibre GL automatically requests `@2x` tiles if the style’s `tileSize` is 512 and the device pixel ratio > 1. | Ensure your tile server provides `@2x` tiles (e.g., `.../{z}/{x}/{y}@2x.png`). |
| **Terrain DEM** | If the style references a `raster-dem` source, you must also load the `terrain` property in `maplibreOptions`. | `maplibreOptions: { terrain: { source: 'terrain', exaggeration: 1.5 } }` |
| **Performance on low-end devices** | WebGL rendering is cheap, but many vector layers can still be heavy. | Keep the number of layers low, use `maxzoom`/`minzoom` on sources, simplify geometries, or switch to raster tiles for static imagery. |
| **Offline / air-gapped** | The plugin works with any source that can be read by MapLibre GL, including **PMTiles** (single-file archives). | Use the `maplibre-pmtiles-patterns` skill to serve a `pmtiles://` URL, then reference it in the style. |
| **Layer order** | By default the GL canvas sits **below** any Leaflet vector layers you add later. If you need a Leaflet overlay *under* the basemap (rare), add it **before** creating the GL layer. | `L.geoJSON(...).addTo(map); // under` then `new MapLibreGL(...).addTo(map);` |
| **Map rotation / pitch** | Leaflet is 2-D only; the plugin disables MapLibre’s pitch/bearing sync. | If you need 3-D terrain, you must use a pure MapLibre GL map (no Leaflet). The plugin will ignore `bearing`/`pitch` changes. |

---

## 6️⃣  Optional Extras

### 6.1 Using **PMTiles** for a single-file vector tile archive

If you want an offline-first solution (e.g., a kiosk), generate a PMTiles archive (`mytiles.pmtiles`) and serve it with a static file server or via the `pmtiles://` protocol.

```js
// Assuming you have installed the pmtiles library
import { PMTiles } from '@maplibre/pmtiles';

const pmtiles = new PMTiles('https://example.com/mytiles.pmtiles');
mapLibreLayer.getMap().addSource('offline', {
  type: 'vector',
  url: 'pmtiles://mytiles.pmtiles',
});
```

> See the **maplibre-pmtiles-patterns** skill for a full walkthrough.

### 6.2 Adding a **raster-DEM** for hillshading

```js
const glMap = mapLibreLayer.getMap();

glMap.addSource('terrain', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'https://tileserver.example.com/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
  tileSize: 256,
  maxzoom: 14,
});

glMap.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain', exaggeration: 1.5 });
```

Now you can add a hillshade layer:

```js
glMap.addLayer({
  id: 'hillshade',
  type: 'hillshade',
  source: 'terrain',
  paint: {
    'hillshade-shadow-color': '#000',
    'hillshade-highlight-color': '#fff',
    'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.5,
  },
});
```

### 6.3 Styling the basemap at runtime

If you need to hide the basemap’s label layer so your own labels are clearer, you can toggle visibility:

```js
glMap.setLayoutProperty('road-label', 'visibility', 'none'); // hide road labels
```

Or insert a custom layer **before** the first symbol layer:

```js
// Find the first symbol layer in the style
const firstSymbol = glMap.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol');
glMap.addLayer(customLayer, firstSymbol.id); // inserts just before it
```

